        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Suppose we want to find the integral of a power function multiplied by a constant, like <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5x^2.]</span> How do we proceed?</p>",
            "readable_text": "Suppose we want to find the integral of a power function multiplied by a constant, like [MATH: 5x^2.] How do we proceed?",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>According to the <strong>constant factor rule</strong>, we can just take the constant factor out of the integral, like this:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\int 5 x^2\\, \\textrm{d}x = 5\\cdot \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "According to the constant factor rule, we can just take the constant factor out of the integral, like this:\n[MATH: \\int 5 x^2\\, \\textrm{d}x = 5\\cdot \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Then, we solve the integral using the power rule:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Then, we solve the integral using the power rule:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5 \u22c5 \u222bx^{2}dx|= 5 \u22c5 ((x^{2 + 1})/(2 + 1)) + C; = (5)/(3)x^{3} + C]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: 5 \u22c5 \u222bx^{2}dx|= 5 \u22c5 ((x^{2 + 1})/(2 + 1)) + C; = (5)/(3)x^{3} + C]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We can use this trick in general. Whenever the integrand is a function <span class=\"math\">[MATH: f(x)]</span> multiplied by a constant <span class=\"math\">[MATH: k,]</span> we can simplify as</p>",
            "readable_text": "We can use this trick in general. Whenever the integrand is a function [MATH: f(x)] multiplied by a constant [MATH: k,] we can simplify as",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\int k f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x = k \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\int k f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x = k \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong>Watch out!</strong> We can only take <em>constant factors</em> out of integrals. We <em>cannot</em> take any variables out of integrals.</p>",
            "readable_text": "Watch out! We can only take constant factors out of integrals. We cannot take any variables out of integrals.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nSuppose we want to find the integral of a power function multiplied by a constant, like [MATH: 5x^2.] How do we proceed?\n\nContent:\nAccording to the constant factor rule, we can just take the constant factor out of the integral, like this:\n[MATH: \\int 5 x^2\\, \\textrm{d}x = 5\\cdot \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x]\n\nContent:\nThen, we solve the integral using the power rule:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 5 \u22c5 \u222bx^{2}dx|= 5 \u22c5 ((x^{2 + 1})/(2 + 1)) + C; = (5)/(3)x^{3} + C]\n\nContent:\nWe can use this trick in general. Whenever the integrand is a function [MATH: f(x)] multiplied by a constant [MATH: k,] we can simplify as\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\int k f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x = k \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.]\n\nContent:\nWatch out! We can only take constant factors out of integrals. We cannot take any variables out of integrals."
      },
