          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \u222b6dx|= 6\u222b1dx; = 6\u222bx^{0}dx; = 6 \u22c5 (x^{0 + 1})/(0 + 1) + C; = 6 \u22c5 x + C; = 6x + C]</span></p><p>In general, we can see that for any constant <span class=\"math\">[MATH: k,]</span> we have <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\displaystyle \\int k \\,\\textrm d x = kx + C.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n[MATH: \u222b6dx|= 6\u222b1dx; = 6\u222bx^{0}dx; = 6 \u22c5 (x^{0 + 1})/(0 + 1) + C; = 6 \u22c5 x + C; = 6x + C]\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant [MATH: k,] we have [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int k \\,\\textrm d x = kx + C.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
