    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nSuppose we want to find the integral of a power function multiplied by a constant, like [MATH: 5x^2.] How do we proceed?\n\nContent:\nAccording to the constant factor rule, we can just take the constant factor out of the integral, like this:\n[MATH: \\int 5 x^2\\, \\textrm{d}x = 5\\cdot \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x]\n\nContent:\nThen, we solve the integral using the power rule:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 5 \u22c5 \u222bx^{2}dx|= 5 \u22c5 ((x^{2 + 1})/(2 + 1)) + C; = (5)/(3)x^{3} + C]\n\nContent:\nWe can use this trick in general. Whenever the integrand is a function [MATH: f(x)] multiplied by a constant [MATH: k,] we can simplify as\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\int k f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x = k \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.]\n\nContent:\nWatch out! We can only take constant factors out of integrals. We cannot take any variables out of integrals.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Power Function\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2}{3} x \\,\\textrm d x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n[MATH: \u222b(2)/(3)xdx|= (2)/(3)\u222bxdx; = (2)/(3)((x^{1 + 1})/(1 + 1)) + C; = (2)/(3) \u22c5 (x^{2})/(2) + C; = (1)/(3)x^{2} + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u222b(y^{2})/(2)dy.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (y^{3})/(2) + C]\nb. [MATH: (y^{3})/(6) + C]\nc. [MATH: y + C]\nd. [MATH: (y^{3})/(2) + (C)/(2)]\ne. [MATH: (y^{3})/(3) + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u222b(\u2212 4z)dz.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 4z^{2} + C]\nb. [MATH: 2z^{2} + C]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 4 + C]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 2z^{2} \u2212 4C]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 2z^{2} + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Constant Function\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int 6 \\,\\textrm d x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n[MATH: \u222b6dx|= 6\u222b1dx; = 6\u222bx^{0}dx; = 6 \u22c5 (x^{0 + 1})/(0 + 1) + C; = 6 \u22c5 x + C; = 6x + C]\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant [MATH: k,] we have [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int k \\,\\textrm d x = kx + C.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u222b(\u2212 2)dy.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: y + C]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 2y + C]\nc. [MATH: 0]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(y^{2}) + C]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(y) + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u222bedx.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(x) + C]\nb. [MATH: (x)/(e) + C]\nc. [MATH: e^{x} + C]\nd. [MATH: (e^{2})/(2) + C]\ne. [MATH: ex + C]"
